.TH DUP 2
.SH NAME
dup, dup2 \- duplicate an open file descriptor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "dup(fildes)"
.B "int fildes;"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "dup2(fildes, fildes2)"
.B "int fildes, fildes2;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Given a file descriptor returned from an
.BR open ,
.BR pipe ,
or
.BR creat (2)
call,
.B dup
allocates another file descriptor synonymous with the original.
The new
file descriptor is returned.
.B dup
always returns the lowest available file
descriptor.
.PP
In the second form of the call,
.I fildes
is a file descriptor referring to an
open file, and
.I fildes2
is a non-negative integer less than the maximum
value allowed for file descriptors (approximately 14).
.B dup2
causes
.I fildes2
to refer to the same file as
.IR fildes .
If
.I fildes2
already referred to an open
file, it is closed first.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
creat(2), open(2), close(2), pipe(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The value \-1 is returned if: the given file descriptor is invalid; there are
already too many open files.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(dup = 41.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
R0 = file descriptor
R1 = new file descriptor
sys dup
R0 = file descriptor
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 41
AX = fildes
DX = new fildes
int 0Xf1
.fi
.PP
The
.B dup2
entry is implemented by adding 0100 to
.IR fildes .
.sp
